# Canonical identities for git's own tooling.
#
# SCOPE — read this before assuming it fixed something:
#
#   .mailmap is applied by `git shortlog`, `git log --author`, `git blame` and
#   most local tooling. It is NOT applied by GitHub's contributors graph, which
#   links a commit to an account solely by matching the commit's author email to
#   a verified email on that account. Nothing in this file changes what GitHub
#   shows.
#
# WHY IT IS HERE ANYWAY:
#
#   About 2,650 commits on main were authored under addresses that resolve to no
#   GitHub account — `david@getempirica.com` (1299), `empirica@aiworkhorse.local`
#   (1246), `yogapad@local` (111) — while only the 283 authored as
#   `64048715+Nubaeon@users.noreply.github.com` are linked. So `git shortlog -sn`
#   reported one person as five contributors. This file makes the local record
#   true, which is worth having even though the hosted graph is unaffected.
#
# THE HOSTED GRAPH, HONESTLY:
#
#   Only two things would fix it retroactively. (1) Adding the address as a
#   verified email on the account — the supported path, and the only
#   non-destructive one. (2) Rewriting author emails across history, which
#   changes every SHA on every branch, invalidates existing clones, and breaks
#   any external reference to a commit. Not a trade worth making on a repo with
#   active contributors and open PRs.
#
#   Commits authored from 2026-08-01 onward use soulentheo@gmail.com and link
#   correctly without any of this.

